q BT 65.20 771.39 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.661 Tw (By Adrian Cole Stories -- the full story. The reels show only the beginning; the ending lives) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 750.98 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (here.) Tj ET Q
BT /F4 12.00 Tf ET
q BT 65.20 721.50 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.145 Tw (It was a Thursday night at the Marquee, the Don's own club, when the band stopped playing and) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 701.09 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (a young singer took the stage.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 671.61 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.196 Tw (She wore a black evening dress, and around her neck hung a silver necklace with a small locket.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 651.20 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.592 Tw (She looked out at the room, at the men in their suits and the women in their diamonds, and she) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 630.79 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (began to sing.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 601.31 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (Don Adrian Cole froze.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 571.83 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.797 Tw (He had not heard that song in thirty years. His wife had written it, long ago, on a piano in this) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 551.42 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.859 Tw (very building -- and she had never sung it for anyone. Not for a crowd. Not for a recording. It) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 531.01 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (was hers alone, and when she died, the song had died with her.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 501.53 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.861 Tw ("Nobody sings that song," the Don said, rising from his table. His voice cut through the room,) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 481.12 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (and the singer stopped. "My wife wrote it -- and she never sang it for anyone.") Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 451.64 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.350 Tw (The singer looked down at him from the stage. She was twenty-six years old, with long dark) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 431.23 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (wavy hair, and she was not afraid.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 401.75 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg ("She sang it for me," she said. "Every night, before bed.") Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 372.27 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.374 Tw (The room went silent. The Don walked slowly toward the stage, his men falling back behind) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 351.86 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (him.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 322.38 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg ("Who are you?" he asked.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 292.90 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.656 Tw (The singer held up the silver necklace. It caught the light, and the Don's face went pale. He) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 272.49 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (knew that necklace. He had given it to his wife himself, on their wedding day.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 243.01 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg ("My wife died thirty years ago," he said. "She left nothing.") Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 213.53 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg ("Then why," the singer asked, "does this necklace have your name inside it?") Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 184.05 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 0.403 Tw (Her name was Nina Reyes, and she had come to the club for one reason: to find the man whose) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 163.64 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (name was inside the locket.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 134.16 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.035 Tw (The Don took her to his study that night, and there, under the lamplight, he opened the locket) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 113.75 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg 1.092 Tw (himself. Inside, behind the tiny engraved name, lay something he had never expected: a small) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 93.35 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg (brass key.) Tj ET Q
q BT 65.20 63.86 Td 0.0784 0.0784 0.0784 rg ("A key," he said quietly. "My name -- and a key.") Tj ET Q
